# Canonical routing. Every internal link, canonical tag and sitemap entry on
# this site uses the extensionless URL, so the .html form is only ever reached
# from outside. The 301! rules below fold it back onto the canonical URL; the
# matching 200 rules serve the file behind that URL in one hop, with no
# redirect.
#
# The force flag (!) matters and so does its absence. Without it a rule is
# shadowed by a real file, so it never fires for a page that exists on disk.
# With it the rule wins -- for every method, including POST. That is why no
# page whose form posts back to its own .html path may carry a 301! (see the
# notes on /book.html and /commercial.html below).
#
# Trailing slashes need no rules of their own: Netlify normalizes the request
# path at the edge before redirect rules run, so /privacy/ matches the
# /privacy rule and every clean URL is served identically with or without the
# slash. Rules that try to flip a slash on or off would loop forever, so the
# only directory-form rules here are the two that point a directory-style path
# at a genuinely different page (see the notes on /handyman/ and /services/).

# Keep the public reviews collection endpoint stable even when function path
# configuration is not applied by a local or plugin-hosted Netlify runtime.
# Detail and photo routes keep their more-specific function path declarations.
/api/reviews /.netlify/functions/reviews 200

/index.html /                    301!

# /gallery was removed from the site; its URL folds onto the reviews page,
# which now carries the customer photos in a lightbox of its own.
/gallery.html /reviews           301!
/gallery     /reviews           301!

# /404.html is a real page (Netlify serves it automatically with a 404 status
# for any path that matches nothing), but its .html form still folds onto the
# clean URL like every other page.
/404.html   /404           301!
/404        /404.html      200

/privacy.html /privacy            301!
/privacy    /privacy.html    200
# /book.html deliberately has no 301! rule: its own Netlify booking form used
# to post back to /book.html (see git history -- bookings run through the
# embedded Google Calendar scheduler now, but careers, customer-care, services
# and commercial still post to their own paths), and a
# forced redirect answers that POST with a 301 before Netlify Forms ever sees
# it. fetch() then follows the redirect as a GET, the submission is discarded,
# and nothing surfaces the failure. The canonical tag on book.html is what
# points crawlers at /book instead.
/book       /book.html       200
/terms.html /terms            301!
/terms      /terms.html      200
/reviews    /reviews.html    200
# /careers.html and /customer-care.html deliberately have no 301! rules:
# public/js/careers-page.js and public/js/customer-care-page.js each post their
# form back to their own .html path, so a forced redirect would swallow the
# submission the same way it would on /book.html. Their canonical tags point
# crawlers at the clean URLs.
/careers    /careers.html    200
/rates      /rates.html      200
/guarantee  /guarantee.html  200
# /services.html deliberately has no 301! rule: public/js/home-care-plans.js
# posts the home-care subscription to /services.html as a mirror of its API
# submission, and a forced redirect would discard it exactly as described for
# /book.html above. The canonical tag on services.html points crawlers at
# /services.
/services   /services.html   200
/service-areas /service-areas.html 200
/contact    /contact.html    200
/customer-care /customer-care.html 200
/pricing-policy /pricing-policy.html 200
/ai-estimate /ai-estimate.html 200
/emergency.html /emergency        301!
/emergency  /emergency.html 200

# /commercial has no on-disk file at its clean URL -- only commercial.html
# exists -- so this rewrite is what serves /commercial at all. It must never
# carry a 301! : commercial.html's own Netlify Form posts back to the page in
# place (no action attribute, so the browser resubmits to whatever URL it was
# served from), and /commercial.html is left unforced for the same
# POST-shadowing reason as /book.html above. The canonical tag on
# commercial.html points crawlers at /commercial.
/commercial /commercial.html 200

# /offline is the service worker's fallback for navigations that fail with no
# cached copy (see OFFLINE_URL in public/sw.js). Rewritten with a 200 so the
# URL stays /offline, matching the sitemap entry.
/offline    /offline.html    200

/handyman/  /service-areas   301!
/handyman/* /handyman/:splat.html 200

/services/  /services        301!
/services/* /services/:splat.html 200
